g $y=f(x)$ and $y\\prime=f\\prime(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement.\n- **III.** The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation." 13194139537008 17592186045443 true] [17592186577313 1072 #uuid "a098865b-a0f4-5d9b-a304-bd75a306b510" 13194139537008 17592186045443 true] [17592186577313 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in an interval where the function is defined and the equation is valid.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x)=3 \\ln{\\left(4 x + 4 \\right)},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\prime(x)&=\\left(3 \\ln{\\left(4 x + 4 \\right)}\\right)\\prime \\\\ &=3\\cdot\\frac{4}{4 x + 4} \\\\ &=\\frac{12}{4 x + 4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Substituting $y=3 \\ln{\\left(4 x + 4 \\right)}$ and $y\\prime=\\frac{12}{4 x + 4}$ into the original equation gives expressions that are not identical:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\prime &\\overset{?}{=}6 e^{- \\frac{y}{3}} \\\\ \\frac{12}{4 x + 4} &\\overset{?}{=}6e^{-(3 \\ln{\\left(4 x + 4 \\right)})/(3)} \\\\ \\frac{12}{4 x + 4} &\\overset{?}{=}6e^{-\\ln(4 x + 4)} \\\\ \\frac{12}{4 x + 4} &\\not\\equiv \\frac{3}{2 \\left(x + 1\\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the function $y=3 \\ln{\\left(4 x + 4 \\right)}$ is not a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537008 17592186045443 true] [17592186577314 1080 "false" 13194139537008 17592186045443 true] [17592186577314 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139537008 17592186045443 true] [17592186577314 1083 #uuid "a3b54604-0657-55bd-93ff-29802c0d79a9" 13194139537008 17592186045443 true] [17592186577315 1075 #edb/ref 17592186577302 13194139537008 17592186045443 true] [17592186577315 1075 #edb/ref 17592186577303 13194139537008 17592186045443 true] [17592186577315 1076 #edb/